AB  - With the rapid development of Vehicle-to-Everything (V2X) technologies, real-time interactions among vehicles, roadside units, pedestrians, and communication networks have become a vital component of intelligent transportation systems (ITS). However, the high connectivity and complexity of V2X systems also introduce significant cybersecurity risks. Attackers who can manipulate packets or mislead system decisions pose considerable threats to vehicle safety. Recently, Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated exceptional capabilities in natural language processing and code generation, showing potential for generating adversarial attacks. This study develops a simulation framework based on Taiwan's TCROS standard and Eclipse-MOSAIC, integrating LLMs to automate the generation of malicious packets. By utilizing prompt engineering and Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), the framework enables LLMs to comprehend and alter legitimate TCROS packets for adversarial purposes. Four attack scenarios are designed to evaluate the LLMs' ability to modify packets, recover from errors, achieve success rates, and assess their impact on simulated traffic. Results indicate that, with appropriate prompt design and domain knowledge, LLMs can effectively generate structurally valid attack packets that disrupt system behavior. This research validates the potential of LLMs as cybersecurity threats in V2X systems and offers new insights and datasets for designing future defense mechanisms.
