AB  - Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate significant potential for generating and verifying Hardware Description Languages (HDLs) in Electronic Design Automation. However, a notable gap exists in VHDL code generation compared to other HDLs, exacerbated by the absence of an open-source benchmark. To address this gap, we propose SVVHD, an open-source benchmark for evaluating LLMs on VHDL code generation. The first part of SVVHD is an evaluation dataset, constructed by translating 156 diverse Verilog problems into VHDL, employing prompt refactoring and rigorous code equivalence verification. The second part of SVVHD is a set of automated testbenches that perform functional verification of candidate VHDL designs against golden solutions within a sandbox environment. Using SVVHD, we conduct zero-shot code generation evaluations with various LLMs to establish baseline performance for the VHDL code generation domain. Subsequently, we construct both a refactored training set and a basic training set for model fine-tuning, which yield significant performance improvements. The introduction of SVVHD, combined with our fine-tuning methodology, contributes to advancing research in LLM-assisted automated VHDL design.
ER  - 

