AB  - Large language models are increasingly used by students to solve programming problems. However, the quality of the code varies significantly depending on the chosen model and the prompting strategy, particularly across difficulty levels. This paper presents a structured empirical evaluation of promptmodel combinations for Python code generation. A controlled dataset of 192 samples was constructed using twelve LeetCode problems spanning three difficulty levels, four prompting strategies, and four LLMs. Code quality was assessed using an NLP-based competency scoring framework comprising four dimensions: syntax and language use, algorithmic reasoning, data structure usage, Memory and time efficiency. For each problem, the prompt-model pair with the highest aggregated score was designated as the optimal combination, enabling supervised label generation at the problem level. A Random Forest classifier was then trained to predict optimal prompt-model pairs based on competency features and contextual attributes. Experimental results demonstrate the feasibility of learning using difficulty-aware prompt-model selection patterns, providing a foundation for a recommendation framework for LLM-assisted programming.
ER  - 

