AB  - Code translation converts source code from one programming language to another while preserving functional behavior, and is critical for legacy migration and cross-platform development. Recent prompt-based LLM approaches such as UniTrans and TRANSAGENT show promise, but their repair stage relies almost exclusively on runtime test feedback, which is insufficient for diagnosing uncompilable code or silent structural errors. We propose MERIT (Multi-Signal Error Repair via Intelligent Translation), a repair framework that jointly exploits four complementary signals: abstract syntax tree (AST) structural comparison against a same-language reference, a language knowledge base, error pattern matching, and LLM self-reflection. Across TransCoder and HumanEval-X, six translation directions among Python, Java, and C++, and five LLMs of varying capability (Claude Sonnet 4.6, Grok-4, Grok-3, GPT-4.1, and CodeLlama-7B), MERIT consistently improves every model, yielding relative gains of $\mathbf{1. 3 \% - 1 3. 1 \%}$ in compilation accuracy ($\boldsymbol{C A}$) and $\mathbf{1. 3 \% - 2 5. 7 \%}$ in execution accuracy $(E A)$, and it outperforms the single-signal baseline UniTrans by 3.0-3.6 percentage points on CodeLlama-7B under identical settings. A leave-one-out ablation, an ordersensitivity incremental ablation, and an error-cause analysis jointly confirm that the four signals are complementary and nonredundant. Multi-signal repair is therefore substantially more effective than single-signal repair, and MERIT is model-agnostic and easy to deploy.
ER  - 

