AB  - Large language models can synthesize code from prose, but free-form prompting suits enterprise scaffolding poorly: one conceptual change must span several architectural layers, obey an existing repository’s conventions, and remain mutually consistent. We present a metadata-driven code-generation subsystem deployed within Nedo Studio, a model-driven low-code platform. It partitions target files into architecturally coherent units behind a job-stable cacheable prefix, grounds generation at convention-derived repository paths under a path-containment check, and delivers reviewable pull requests. We derive a cost model pricing the provider’s cache-write premium and read discount, showing that cache alignment lowers the marginal billing rate of each unit rather than making repeated-prefix cost constant. On two greenfield fixtures at five repetitions, a single-variable counterfactual rebilling of the same recorded requests estimates a 26.6% reduction in billable input after cold-start correction, exactly matching the predicted prefix saving. Normalized by output volume, the latency advantage disappears. A deployment case study shows the generated backend and frontend building, running standalone, and passing scripted create, read, update, and delete checks after four disclosed manual steps, one correcting a generated defect that type checking caught. On a second, open-weight model the pipeline ran unchanged, including the frontend and six-entity jobs; a prompt variant neutralizing copied worked examples markedly improved fidelity and compilation, unevenly and less as jobs grew. A deterministic template baseline wins greenfield scaffolding by construction; on a fixture supplying a house convention, only a wider grounding scope satisfied it, on both models.
