AB  - Large language model (LLM) applications not only require infrastructure observability but also incorporate aspects like prompts, tools, retrieval, and agent orchestration, resulting in observability needs that go beyond what infrastructure metrics can provide. This paper introduces an LLM application tracing reference architecture using OpenTelemetry, which consists of two spans: the root span, the chain span, and the model span and tool span. The architecture ties operational signals such as latency, token usage, throughput and errors to evaluation signals such as hallucination detection, JSON validity, and semantic similarity. The pipeline has a dual path with low latency telemetry going to the trace and metrics backends and replicated traces going to an asynchronous evaluation worker. Precision, recall and F1 of the hallucination detector on a labeled set of 200 completions are 0.88, 0.81 and 0.84 respectively in the evaluated reference configuration. Under the reported load configuration, the telemetry pipeline was able to process up to 8,500 spans/s and 12,000 metrics/s. These results will provide a characterization of the tested reference deployment and should not be taken as universal production guarantees. The framework offers a traceable foundation to improve, evaluate, and debug LLM applications.
ER  - 

