AB  - Learning-based vulnerability detectors, particularly those built on pre-trained code models, are increasingly used in software assurance, yet they remain susceptible to semantics-preserving adversarial transformations. Although such attacks help expose and mitigate model weaknesses, existing methods use narrow transformation spaces, lack fine-grained guidance, or fragment code to circumvent large language model (LLM) safeguards, increasing detector queries and risking broken vulnerability-relevant dependencies. We propose AttaCode, an attribution-guided adversarial code generation scheme that prioritizes transformations using information from the detector and performs jailbreak-enabled LLM generation over complete source code. AttaCode aggregates gradient-based token attributions onto abstract syntax tree nodes to rank 25 semantics-preserving transformations. It then optimizes placeholder-based jailbreak prompts through iterative mutation to reduce safety refusals during subsequent generation, before instantiating them with the full program and dynamically selected transformations for iterative adversarial generation. Extensive experiments show that AttaCode outperforms state-of-the-art (SOTA) baselines, with an average attack success rate (ASR) above 80% while requiring 75%–96% fewer detector queries. It also achieves the highest ASR on average at every evaluated detector query budget. Its transformations preserve tested behavior for 89.9% of programs after three sequential edits, receive favorable naturalness ratings, and yield the lowest automated detection rates. Moreover, adversarial fine-tuning improves detector accuracy by 9.56–12.52 percentage points.
